<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation for every project, test projects included.
    (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because the SDK locks in its
    decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)

    The test project used to opt out. That made IDE0005 structurally unable
    to fire there, because documentation generation is its documented
    prerequisite (roslyn#41640) - halheinrich/Math#43. It now inherits this
    value and suppresses CS1591 instead, so the baseline means one thing in
    both projects rather than two things depending on which you are in.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every ProjectReference builds in the configuration its consumer was
    asked for. halheinrich/Math#47.

    Without this, a Release build of this member links the *Debug*
    BigRationalLibrary, and every "0 warnings in Release" and "passes in
    Release" ever recorded here was measured against a Debug producer.
    Measured in this member, all four rows a solution build at
    `-c Release -t:Rebuild`, producer identified by content hash (its
    Release is 8dc523aee5947338, its Debug 2eb25db84fa69872):

      1  pristine                                    2eb25db8  Debug
      2  ItemDefinitionGroup with SetConfiguration    2eb25db8  Debug
      3  that ItemDefinitionGroup plus this property  8dc523ae  Release
      4  this property alone                         8dc523ae  Release

    Rows 3 and 4 flip one thing and the effect reverses, so the metadata
    approach that looks like the obvious fix is a no-op in the strongest
    sense: it changes nothing, including the bug. It is applied correctly
    at evaluation and then overwritten at build. In
    Microsoft.Common.CurrentVersion.targets on SDK 10.0.400, line 1584
    defaults ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform to true whenever
    BuildingSolutionFile or BuildingInsideVisualStudio is set, and line
    1609 hands it to the AssignProjectConfiguration task, whose job is
    then to rewrite SetConfiguration on every reference absent from the
    solution map, unsetting the parent's configuration so the referenced
    project falls to its own default. A solution assigns a configuration
    only to the projects it contains, and this one lists only its own two.

    Line 1584's condition also names BuildingInsideVisualStudio, so this
    was never confined to `dotnet build <solution>`: Release builds inside
    Visual Studio linked the Debug producer too.

    Flagged rather than quietly relied on. This overrides an MSBuild
    default instead of working with it, and the property is not documented
    public surface; it lives in the SDK's targets, which is where it was
    read. The default exists so a solution configuration is not forced
    onto a project that may not define it. Every member here defines
    exactly Debug and Release with no platform variation, so the condition
    the default guards against does not arise. If that ever changes, this
    is the decision to revisit.

    Preferred over listing BigRationalLibrary in this member's solution,
    which also works: membership scales with dependency depth, has to be
    re-established every time the graph grows, and when incomplete it
    fails silently, restoring exactly this bug with exactly this symptom.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>false</ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was gated on ContinuousIntegrationBuild,
    under a comment reading "only enabled in CI so local `dotnet add package`
    keeps working". This repository has no .github/workflows directory and no
    ref in its history ever contained one, so there was no CI for the gate to
    be enabled in: the sentence named a tradeoff between local convenience and
    a CI guarantee where only the convenience existed. The gate was not dormant
    pending a workflow, it was never on anywhere, and the comment implied
    otherwise. Nor does the SDK infer the property from a CI environment,
    measured on 10.0.400 in two sibling members: it evaluates empty even with
    CI and GITHUB_ACTIONS set.

    A file whose promise depends on a switch set somewhere else cannot state
    its own promise. Removing the condition makes the promise this file's own,
    which is the reason to prefer it over waiting for a workflow to carry it.

    What it guards here is now what it guards everywhere else, and what it no
    longer guards is recorded because the loss looks worse than it is. Until
    migration step 7 (halheinrich/Math#10) this member took BigRational by
    PackageReference, so the lock recorded that edge as "type": "Direct" with a
    requested range and a resolved version — exactly what locked mode compares
    — and this was the one member where the gate reached it. The edge is a
    ProjectReference now, and halheinrich/Math#44 measured what that costs: a
    project-edge constraint is written into the lock and never read, so an
    impossible range on it passes restore in silence. The protection and the
    hazard left together, though, because a path reference carries no version
    to drift. Nobody should read this as a regression, or write a mechanism to
    guard it.

    The library project consequently carries no PackageReference at all. What
    this file still enforces is the test project's tooling graph — xunit, the
    test SDK, its VS runner and coverlet — where a version genuinely can move
    and locked mode genuinely compares it.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now fails the restore with
    NU1004 instead of quietly rewriting the lock. Recover with
    `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`, which is what the CLI's
    force-evaluate switch sets, and commit the regenerated lock. One command,
    once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole purpose is that it
    does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here as a property
    because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.) NuGet writes the
    regenerated lock with CRLF while .gitattributes pins it to LF, so
    `git status` then reports a change that `git diff` cannot show.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
